<DocsLayout title="Headless | react-data-table-component" description="Use the logic layer of react-data-table-component without its default markup or styles: what the headless hooks are, how they fit together, and when to use them.">

  <h1>Headless</h1>

  <p>
    <code>react-data-table-component</code> ships the hooks (and, for pinning, plain functions) that power
    <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> internally. You can import them directly and compose them yourself when
    you need full control over markup and styles, without giving up sorting, pagination, selection,
    filtering, resizing, pinning, keyboard navigation, and export logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the middle ground between using the styled <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> as-is and rebuilding
    everything from scratch with a fully headless library. The hooks own state and data logic. Markup,
    ARIA roles, and styles are yours to write.
  </p>

  <h2>Importing</h2>

  <CodeBlock lang="ts" code={`import { useColumns, useTableState, useTableData } from 'react-data-table-component/headless';`} />

  <p>
    The <code>/headless</code> entry contains only the hooks, the pinning helpers, and their types. It does
    not include <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> and does not inject the stylesheet. The same hooks are
    also exported from the main <code>react-data-table-component</code> entry, which is convenient when you
    already render a <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> elsewhere, but that entry always loads the component
    and its CSS.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>When to use this</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>You have a design system and can't override the default styles far enough</li>
    <li>You're building inside a heavily customized UI (e.g. a design system component library)</li>
    <li>You want the table logic but need to render it inside something other than a standard <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> or flex layout</li>
    <li>You want to compose only some hooks (e.g. sorting + pagination) and handle the rest yourself</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If the default <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> with <code>customStyles</code> covers your needs, use that. It is less work.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2>The data pipeline</h2>

  <p>
    Each hook owns one layer of the pipeline. Data flows top-to-bottom; state flows back up via callbacks.
    Understanding this makes it straightforward to drop any layer and replace it with your own logic.
  </p>

  <div class="mermaid-diagram">
    <pre class="mermaid" set:html={pipelineDiagram} />
  </div>

  <h2>Component tree</h2>

  <p>
    When you use <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code>, it renders the following structure. Each node has a stable CSS
    class so you can target it from your stylesheet. When you use the hooks directly, you own this tree entirely.
  </p>

  <div class="mermaid-diagram">
    <pre class="mermaid" set:html={componentTreeDiagram} />
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code>'s ARIA roles switch from <code>table</code>/<code>cell</code> to
    <code>grid</code>/<code>gridcell</code> when <code>cellNavigation</code> is enabled, adding keyboard
    grid navigation on top of the same tree. The <code>useCellNavigation</code> hook below is the same
    logic that powers it. Compose it into your own markup to get the same roving-tabindex behavior. See
    <a href="/docs/keyboard-navigation">Keyboard navigation</a> for the interaction pattern it implements.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>The core pipeline hooks</h2>

  <DocsTable
    headers={['Hook', 'What it does']}
    rows={[
      ['<code>useColumns</code>', 'Manages column order and drag-to-reorder'],
      ['<code>useTableState</code>', 'Sort, pagination, and row-selection state + dispatchers'],
      ['<code>useTableData</code>', 'Sorts and paginates the raw data into renderable rows'],
      ['<code>useColumnFilter</code>', 'Per-column filter values and client-side filter logic'],
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Feature hooks</h2>

  <p>Compose any of these on top of the pipeline as needed. None of them depend on each other.</p>

  <DocsTable
    headers={['Hook', 'What it does']}
    rows={[
      ['<code>useColumnVisibility</code>', 'Show/hide columns by id, independent of the sort/filter pipeline'],
      ['<code>useColumnResize</code>', 'Drag-to-resize column widths (pointer-driven: mouse, touch, and pen)'],
      ['<code>useCellNavigation</code>', 'Keyboard grid navigation: arrows, Home/End, roving tabindex. Same logic behind <code>cellNavigation</code>'],
      ['<code>useTableExport</code>', 'Generates CSV / JSON from columns and rows, with download + clipboard helpers'],
      ['<code>getPinnedOffsets</code> / <code>getPinnedTotalWidths</code> / <code>getPinnedCellMeta</code>', 'Pure functions (not hooks) that compute sticky-column offsets and shadow metadata for <code>column.pinned</code>'],
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    None of them render JSX or load CSS. A few return class names or read <code>data-*</code> attributes
    that you put on your own elements. Those cases are called out in the
    <a href="/docs/headless/reference">hook reference</a>, which has the signature, options, defaults, and
    return value of each one.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Basic example</h2>

  <p>
    For a complete, styled version with row selection, see the
    <a href="/docs/headless/tailwind">Headless table with Tailwind</a> example.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="tsx" code={`import {
  useColumns,
  useTableState,
  useTableData,
  useColumnFilter,
  SortOrder,
  type TableColumn,
} from 'react-data-table-component/headless';

interface Person {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  role: string;
}

const columns: TableColumn<Person>[] = [
  { id: 'name', name: 'Name', selector: row => row.name, sortable: true },
  { id: 'email', name: 'Email', selector: row => row.email },
  { id: 'role', name: 'Role', selector: row => row.role },
];

function MyTable({ data }: { data: Person[] }) {
  // 1. Column order + default sort
  const { tableColumns, defaultSortColumn, defaultSortDirection } = useColumns(columns, {
    defaultSortFieldId: 'name',
  });

  // 2. Sort / page / selection state. Only \`data\` is required.
  const { tableState, handleSort, handleChangePage } = useTableState({
    data,
    defaultSortColumn,
    defaultSortDirection,
  });

  const { selectedColumn, sortDirection, sortColumns, currentPage, rowsPerPage } = tableState;

  // 3. Sorted rows
  const { sortedData } = useTableData({
    data,
    selectedColumn,
    sortDirection,
    sortColumns,
    currentPage,
    rowsPerPage,
  });

  // 4. Optional: per-column filtering. Filter the full sorted set, then slice the page.
  const { filteredData } = useColumnFilter(tableColumns);
  const filteredRows = filteredData(sortedData);
  const rows = filteredRows.slice((currentPage - 1) * rowsPerPage, currentPage * rowsPerPage);
  const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(filteredRows.length / rowsPerPage));

  return (
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            {tableColumns.map(col => (
              <th
                key={col.id}
                onClick={() =>
                  col.sortable &&
                  handleSort({
                    type: 'SORT_CHANGE',
                    selectedColumn: col,
                    clearSelectedOnSort: false,
                    additive: false,
                    defaultSortDirection: SortOrder.ASC,
                  })
                }
              >
                {col.name}
              </th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((row, i) => (
            <tr key={row.id}>
              {tableColumns.map(col => (
                <td key={col.id}>{col.selector?.(row, i)}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <button onClick={() => handleChangePage(currentPage - 1)} disabled={currentPage <= 1}>
        Previous page
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => handleChangePage(currentPage + 1)} disabled={currentPage >= totalPages}>
        Next page
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}`} />

  <h3>Filter before you paginate</h3>

  <p>
    Run <code>filteredData</code> on <code>sortedData</code> and slice the page yourself, as above. Filtering
    <code>tableRows</code> with <code>pagination: true</code> filters a single page, so a match on page 3 never
    shows while you are on page 1. When a filter changes, call <code>handleChangePage(1)</code>, since the
    narrower result set may not reach the current page.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you don't filter, pass <code>pagination: true</code> to <code>useTableData</code> and render
    <code>tableRows</code>, which is already sliced to the current page.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>How <code>useTableState</code> and <code>useTableData</code> relate</h2>

  <p>
    <code>useTableState</code> owns the sort/page/selection <strong>state</strong>.
    <code>useTableData</code> takes that state as inputs and returns the <strong>derived rows</strong> to render.
    They are intentionally separate so you can skip <code>useTableData</code> entirely if you're handling
    sorting and pagination server-side.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="ts" code={`// Server-side: you manage the rows, just use useTableState for UI state
const { tableState, handleSort, handleChangePage } = useTableState({ ..., paginationServer: true });

// When tableState changes, fire your own API call. No useTableData needed.
useEffect(() => {
  fetchPage(tableState.currentPage, tableState.rowsPerPage, tableState.sortDirection);
}, [tableState.currentPage, tableState.rowsPerPage, tableState.sortDirection]);`} />

  <hr />

  <h2>State ownership</h2>

  <DocsTable
    headers={['State', 'Owned by', 'Controlled prop']}
    rows={[
      ['Current page', '<code>useTableState</code>', '<code>paginationDefaultPage</code> + <code>onChangePage</code>'],
      ['Rows per page', '<code>useTableState</code>', '<code>paginationPerPage</code> + <code>onChangeRowsPerPage</code>'],
      ['Selected rows', '<code>useTableState</code>', '<code>selectableRowSelected</code> + <code>onSelectedRowsChange</code>'],
      ['Sort column + direction', '<code>useTableState</code>', '<code>defaultSortFieldId</code> + <code>onSort</code>'],
      ['Column filters', '<code>useColumnFilter</code>', '<code>filterValues</code> + <code>onFilterChange</code>'],
      ['Column widths', '<code>useColumnResize</code>', '<code>initialColumnWidths</code> + <code>onColumnResize</code>'],
      ['Column order', '<code>useColumns</code>', '<code>onColumnOrderChange</code>'],
      ['Expanded rows', 'local to each row component', '<code>expandableRowExpanded</code> + <code>onRowExpandToggled</code>'],
    ]}
  />

  <hr />

  <h2>What you're responsible for</h2>

  <p>When you use the hooks directly, you own:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Markup</strong>: <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, whatever fits your design system</li>
    <li><strong>Styles</strong>: no CSS is injected; you apply your own classes</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility</strong>: <code>role</code>, <code>aria-sort</code>, <code>aria-selected</code>, screen-reader labels</li>
    <li><strong>Pagination UI</strong>: the hooks give you <code>currentPage</code>, <code>rowsPerPage</code>, and <code>handleChangePage</code>; you render the controls</li>
    <li><strong>Selection UI</strong>: <code>tableState.selectedRows</code>, <code>handleSelectedRow</code>, <code>handleSelectAllRows</code> give you the data; you render checkboxes</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2>Staying on the styled <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code></h2>

  <p>
    None of these exports affect the default <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code>. It continues to work identically.
    The hooks are an additive unlocked door, not a replacement.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Live demo</h2>

  <p>
    The example below is built entirely from the four hooks. No <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> component.
    It has sortable headers, per-column filter inputs, manual pagination controls, and striped rows,
    all in custom markup with no injected CSS.
  </p>

</DocsLayout>

